good morning to you thanks for being  with us um i mean what more would you  want to see the government doing to deal  with this heatwave kit has laid out  there very clearly that there have been  lots of  things put in place to make sure that  services can cope as best as they can  well we think the government ought to do  a number of things first is to uh turn  up for work you your line of questioning  for kit malt house that  in that interview was very uncomfortable  because the prime minister is clearly  clocked off and so many of the ministers  in his government the second thing is  that we think there ought to be a  dedicated minister in the cabinet office  who coordinates an emergency response of  this  nature and the third is that we think  there ought to be a  concerted work from central government  to work with local areas to make sure  that local resilience plans are in place  we saw during the pandemic how many  local areas responded magnificently  rising up to sort out national problems  that couldn't be solved from the centre  but we do have a patchwork of those  emergency responses across the country  and we need some coordination to make  sure that every place has a local  resilience plan in place  to protect us from extreme heat events  like this  so just to be clear you want to see a  dedicated minister to deal with uh  resilience to natural disaster  that's right we've had a number of  national emergencies in recent years  but we're also seeing more extreme  weather events across the country many  parts of the country hit for example by  frequent flooding as well and when  temperatures rise to this level as many  of the scientists and experts have been  saying on your show there are serious  health repercussions not least for  people who are elderly but also for some  people who are extremely fit too so we  need to have a national coordinated  response and we think a dedicated  minister in the cabinet office would be  one way of making sure that this runs  right through the heart of government  and everything that they do  i mean i guess the government response  would say that they have the cobra  committee that deals with these  emergencies but  we'll leave that there for now i mean  specifically on on schools lisa and andy  do you think that they should be uh  closing as kids are having to deal with  some  incredible temperatures in the classroom  i mean i would follow the advice of the  scientists i'm not remotely qualified to  tell um children and teachers  and parents whether it's  um whether whether that advice is right  or not and i would trust the scientists  who say that schools are perfectly safe  as long as precautions are taken and my  own child has gone to school today with  armed with all the things that we've  been told some cream hats  water  schools across the country i know are  relaxing school uniform requirements to  make sure that children can stay cool  and they're following the advice that is  given keeping children out of direct  sunlight and on that basis i see no  reason to disregard the advice from  experts and to follow that advice  involved  i mean would a labour government lisa  nandy bring in uh  perhaps legislation or more um  guidelines proper guidelines that say  that you know i have some friends from  australia that say that when they were  at school no hat no place so they  couldn't go outside in the temperatures  when they were too high if they didn't  have a hat on do you think we should  have more rules like that seeing as the  met office say that these are going to  be  summers like this for the foreseeable  no i think schools take a very common  sense approach to this if a child turns  up without a hat they'll find they'll  find one in lost property or or find a  way to make sure that that child can can  be outside with their friends and stay  stay cool i think pragmatism and common  sense  and following the advice that he's given  is the best way to get through these  events but there's a bigger question as  well of course about why we're seeing  more frequent extreme weather events and  hotter temperatures  and action on climate change making sure  that we're leading the world again after  a lost decade in cutting back our  investment in clean energy seems to me  that's the thing that government ought  to be focusing on right now  so let's move on then to the  conservative leadership race and on that  very subject um you will have seen that  many of the the candidates say they're  going to scrap the net zero targets by  2050 i mean what's what's your response  to that idea  i mean just you know today of all days  it just shows the absurdity of the  leadership contest you've got a group of  candidates all of whom are very much  preoccupied with winning the votes of  conservative mps and 160 000  conservative party members and the  interests of the country have been  completely discarded not only are a  number of the candidates saying that  they want to scrap the commitment to net  zero but leveling up which was the big  idea the mandate on which this  government came to power in 2019 and  turning around the situation where most  people in most places have been written  off and the contribution that they can  make written off by their government for  lack of backing and investment that is  barely featured in the debate that is a  huge betrayal of millions of people who  voted for change and that's why i'm in  darlington today to set out labour's  plans to start putting us putting that  right getting the economy firing on all  cylinders and making sure that all  people in all places can make a  contribution again  well there were some reporting that  perhaps uh leveling up will be rebranded  but the mayor of t side said that rishi  sunak is is committed to it so that will  still continue there will still be this  plan under arishi united government if  he wins  i mean i i found it extraordinary that a  few weeks ago rishi cena was the  chancellor who was  declaring that we would level up the  country and now he's planning to run the  economy like thatcher  many people across the north of england  who will remember the way that our  economy was run under thatcher is the  opposite of what boris johnson and rishi  sunak promised they promised to bring  good jobs back to our community and get  money back into people's pockets and get  the economy firing on all cylinders  we're one of the most geographically  unequal countries in the world and most  parts of the country suffer from chronic  lack of investment that's why we've set  out plans to invest 28 billion a year  every year for a decade in bringing  those good clean energy jobs back to  britain we could tackle climate change  we could get money back into people's  pockets and we could get the economy  firing on all cylinders if we had a  government with ambition for this  country that matched the ambition of the  people in it  and at least nandy you will have seen in  the debate from friday from some of the  coverage over the weekend from the  debate last night that uh the word  socialist is being bandied about is  being used as a stick to beat a number  of the candidates list trust has been  described as socialists rishi soon as  being described as socialist why is that  such a dirty word for the conservative  party  i mean because they this is the most  extreme version of the conservative  party  i've ever seen and because they believe  that only individuals can achieve things  that by working together we do worse and  in that they're completely wrong look at  the recent strikes track action over  public sector pay in wales the welsh  labor government avoided strike action  because with their workforce and they  work with employers as partners that's  one of the reasons why i'm here in  darlington today to set out labour's  plans to work with communities to give  them more powers to be able to generate  revenue and invest it in the things that  matter to them saving local buildings  and high streets and reinvesting in the  people in places that have been  completely written off by this  government we've got a really extreme  contest playing out across the tory  party at the moment and the people have  been cut out of the conversation labour  are determined that we're going to bring  people back into the conversation and  partner with them in order to change  this country for the good  okay lisa nandy shadow leveling up  secretary thank you very much for your  time this morning thank you for being  with us thank you

